xinput1_3: a gamepad's axes and buttons come in two conventions, not one.

The mapping this file had is the one Microsoft's own gamepad descriptor uses,
and the one winexinput builds when it wraps a pad: X/Y for the left stick, Rx/Ry
for the right, Z and Rz for the triggers, buttons numbered A B X Y. The other
convention is at least as common -- every Sony pad follows it -- and puts the
right stick on Z/Rz, the triggers on Rx/Ry, and numbers its buttons in the order
they sit on the face.

Read the wrong way round a pad is not merely imprecise. A trigger rests at zero,
and zero on an axis meant to be centred is full deflection: the right stick sits
jammed in a corner and the game moves without being touched. Measured on a
DualSense Edge, 2026-09-09.

The axes are read in two places -- which caps scale a field, and which usage
feeds it -- and both are changed here; changing one and not the other looks
exactly like changing neither.

The hat is a third case and needs no vendor at all: the table numbers its
directions 1..8 with 0 for centred, which is what winexinput declares, while a
DualSense declares 0..7 and reports 8 for nothing pressed. Read against the
table, a resting D-pad is up-and-left held down for ever. So the value is
normalised against the hat's own logical minimum and anything outside its
declared range is centred, which is right for both conventions.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
